    "result_text": "The full test suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`). Here's what I implemented.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe spec adds a `customerCode` (`<LAST3>-<NNNN>`) assigned to each owner at creation. Since the sequence is \"one more than the current number of owners\" at assign time, the code can't be recomputed later \u2014 so it's **persisted** and returned on `GET`.\n\n**Changes, each in its proper unit:**\n\n1. **New pipeline step** \u2014 `AssignOwnerCustomerCode.java` (single responsibility: compute the sequence and format the code). It reads the count via `ownerRepository.findAll().size() + 1`, formats `<LAST3>-<NNNN>` (upper-cased first \u22643 letters of `lastName`, hyphen, `%04d` sequence), and sets it on the entity.\n\n2. **Wiring** \u2014 `owners.POST.yml`: inserted `assignCustomerCode` between `build` and `save` (under the same `transaction` governance), so the entity exists and the code is persisted.\n\n3. **Domain model** \u2014 `Owner.java`: added the persisted `customerCode` field (`@Column(name = \"customer_code\")`) with getter/setter.\n\n4. **Response contract** \u2014 `openapi.yml`: added `customerCode` as a `readOnly` string on the `Owner` (response) schema, so MapStruct auto-maps `Owner.customerCode \u2192 OwnerDto.customerCode` by name and it surfaces on `GET`. Left `OwnerFields` (the request DTO) untouched, so clients can't supply it.\n\n5. **Schema** \u2014 added `customer_code` to the `owners` table in all four DB scripts (`hsqldb` drives the tests; `h2`/`mysql`/`postgres` kept consistent).\n\nThe `AcceptanceTest` verifies both the format (`SMI-`, `JON-` prefixes) and the global monotonic sequence (`cb == ca + 1`) \u2014 both pass.",
